t1 ZPL Features : Statements

p Programming statements are the main tools for bringing dynamism in your program. \
They are generally present in every programming language under pretty similar forms. \
If you are familiar with programming in general, whatever language you used, \
you should easilly find your way into Z statements.
b

t2 Conditions

t3 Basics

p The most basic statement in every algorithm is the conditionnal execution (commonly called "if"). \
This statement allow your program to execute a certain block of code or not depending on a user defined condition. \
In Z, such statement can be made using the STM_IF_ ZCI :
" z Figure 81
if CONDITION { DO_SOMETHING }
"
p The content between braces includers must be ZCIs. \
It is possible to put nothing in it. \
As for function declarations, this braces includer defines a \c0000ff\bscope\b\c \
so data items declared inside of it are \cff0000\bnot\b\c accessible from the outside. \
However, accessibility of higher scope elements is still possible (as usual).

p Conditions in Z are VALUE ZCEs that can be of any type however the final result will be cashted into the minimal size type which is \mbyt\m. \
The content of the IF statement is only executed if the condition is different from 0 (\m`00\m to be precise). \
In programming in general, we call that kind of binary status values : "boolean". \
In ZPL, we don't have booleans as root types as we said in previous chapters but the type \mboo\m is assumed to be the generic name for a boolean element. \
By the way and as a recall, VALUE literal patterns \mtrue\m and \mfalse\m correspond to the 2 status of a boolean element so they can be used in conditions.
" z Figure 82
//defining type "boo" (may be in a standard library)
typ boo byt

//IF statement always executing its content
if true {
    ...
}

//IF statement never executing its content
if false {
    ...
}

//IF statement executing its content depending on a more variable thing
a = ...
if a {
    ...
}
"

p Comparing to what we saw in previous chapters, this should be quite simple to understand isn't it ? \
Statements in general are not the hardest part of the language. \
Besides, as an everlasting logic, this simplicity turns them into a pretty complex thing in compilation (at least in low-level scope). \
Programming complexity always remains balanced between user's knowledge and the compiler.


t3 Extensions

p IF statements can be extended by an opposite side execution content using the \mels\m keyword (corresponding to "else"). \
This keyword must be followed by another ZCI block given using braces includers. \
This new content will only be executed if the condition is \bNOT\b true.
" z Figure 83
byt a = ...

//IF statement with an "els" extension
if a { SOME_ZCIs } els { SOME_OTHER_ZCIs }

// SOME_ZCIs will be executed if "a" is true (differs from `00)
// SOME_OTHER_ZCIs will be executed if "a" is false (equals `00)
"

p Another extension can also be added to an IF statement to allow a simpler syntax : "else if" blocks. \
Those blocks are pretty similar to the "else" blocks except that they also hold another condition on themselves. \
We use the keyword "elf" to add this extension to an IF statement.
" z Figure 84
byt a = ...

//IF statement with an "elf" extension
if a { SOME ZCIs } elf CONDITION_2 { SOME_OTHER_ZCIs }

// SOME_ZCIs will be executed if "a" is true
// SOME_OTHER_ZCIs will be executed if "a" is false AND if CONDITION_2 is true



//This is exactly the same thing as this :
if a {
    SOME_ZCIs
} els {
    if CONDITION_2 { SOME_OTHER_ZCIs }
}
"

p As explained in this example, "elf" extensions don't bring anything new to the code. \
They can be completely replaced by "if-els" statements nested in each other. \
However, using the "elf" syntax is a way much user friendly in most cases and it often allows compilation optimizations.

p This may not be that obvious because we only used one "elf" extension in our examples. \
Indeed, we can use as many "elf" extensions as we want in an IF statements. \
To do so, we simply repeat the extension pattern as many time as we want.
" z Figure 85
byt a = ...
byt b = ...
byt c = ...

//IF statement with multiple "elf" extensions
if a {
    EXEC1
} elf b {
    EXEC2
} elf c {
    EXEC3
} //and so on...

//Here is a brief explanation of the execution workflow here :
//  a is true => execute EXEC1 and that's it.
//  Else => looking at the rest of the statement
//
//    b is true => execute EXEC2 and that's it.
//    Else => looking at the rest of the statement
//
//      c is true => execute EXEC3
//      Else => do nothing at all
"

p By the way, it is also possible to use both "elf" \uand\u "els" extensions to IF statements. \
However, because "els" extension stands for "the remaining cases", we can only use it once and only at the end of the statement. \
Here is how big an "if-elf-els" statement can be.
" z Figure 86
byt a = ...
byt b = ...
byt c = ...
...

if a {      //initial statement
    EXEC1
} elf b {   //first sub-case
    EXEC2
} elf c {   //second sub-case
    EXEC3
} elf ... { //...
    ...
} els {     //last case (every condition is false => last remaining case)
    EXECN
}
"

p One last thing concerning IF statements : extensions can be made on several ZCIs. \
To better represent conditionnal branching if large IF statements, the ZPL allows you to use extensions of IF statements as completely distinc ZCIs. \
That means 2 syntaxes are allowed for STM_IF_ ZCIs :
" z Figure 87
//here is also an STM_IF_ ZCI
elf CONDITION { CONTENT }

//here is an STM_IF_ as well
els { CONTENT }
"

p Obviousy, because those extensions are related to an initial IF statement, the compiler will raise an error if you declare such things alone. \
You must first give a regular "if CONDITION { EXEC }" before using any extension. \
At compile time, those extensions will be attached to the initial IF statement.

l As a conclusion on this part, here is a last example of the use of IF statements and the advantages of using "elf" extensions as well :
" z Figure 88
byt a = ...
byt b = ...
byt c = ...
byt d = ...

//using "elf" extensions
if a {    //initial statement
    EXEC1
} elf b { //first sub-case (still on the same initial ZCI, at line 7)
    EXEC2
}

elf c {   //second sub-case (here we are on a new ZCI)
    EXEC3
}
elf d {   //thirs sub-case (here we are also on a new ZCI)
    EXEC4
} els {   //last case
    EXECN
}



//WITHOUT "elf" extensions
if a { //inital statement
    EXEC1
} els {
    if b { //first sub-case
        EXEC2
    }

    els {
        if c { //second sub-case
            EXEC3
        }

        els {
            if d { //thirs sub-case
                EXEC4
            }

            els { //last case
                EXECN
            }
        }
    }
}

//CONCLUSION : Use "elf" extensions, it is great !
"


t3 Industrial redirections

p A typical case of using IF statements is when dealing with \uexecution \bredirection\b\u. \
This is a really common thing that we have in lots of situations when making a selection between multiple executions or behaviors. \
Let's take a basic example :
" z Figure 89
SCENE_A = `00
SCENE_B = `01
SCENE_C = `02
SCENE_D = `03
SCENE_E = `04

byt scene = ... //select the scene we are currently in

if scene == SCENE_A {
    EXEC_A //execution if we are currently in scene 1
}

elf scene == SCENE_B {
    EXEC_B //execution if we are currently in scene 2
}

elf scene == SCENE_C {
    EXEC_C //execution if we are currently in scene 3
}

elf scene == SCENE_D {
    EXEC_D //execution if we are currently in scene 4
}

elf scene == SCENE_E {
    EXEC_E //execution if we are currently in scene 5
}

els {
    EXEC_U //execution for an unknown scene
}
"

p In those cases, another Z statement is more approprioate (also really common in other programming languages) : the "Switch". \
In some programming languages you can have it named like "Switch-Case" or "Match-Case" statement but in Z, it is just "Switch". \
This statement consists in using the keyword \mswi\m followed by a braces includer. \
However, unlike IF statements, this braces includer does not defines a sub-zone of ZCIs directly but a more specific pattern :
" z Figure 90
swi VALUE_TO_ANALYZE {
    VALUE1 { EXEC1 }
    VALUE2 { EXEC2 }
    VALUE3 { EXEC3 }
    VALUE4 { EXEC4 }
    ...
    def    { EXECU } //using keyword "def" means "if nothing matches" ("els" equivalent)
                     //"def" is OPTIONNAL
}
"
p Every "VALUE" field is a VALUE ZCE. \
Depending on the value to analyze, the execution will go in one direction or another. \
\cff0000\b\uWARNING :\u\b The redirection will be made by using the CEQ operator (\m==\m). \
Please take care of the types used in your VALUE ZCEs.\c

p This kind of statement is more appropriate in our example of execution redirection (figure 80). \
Moreover, using it instead of "if-elf-els" allows optimizations in low-level compilation. \
As for users, this is also shorter and less risky than using tons of IF extensions (avoiding mistakes in conditions) :
" z Figure 91
SCENE_A = `00
SCENE_B = `01
SCENE_C = `02
SCENE_D = `03
SCENE_E = `04

byt scene = ... //select the scene we are currently in

//same thing as the previous example but using a SWITCH statement
swi scene {
    SCENE_A { EXEC_A }
    SCENE_B { EXEC_B }
    SCENE_C { EXEC_C }
    SCENE_D { EXEC_D }
    SCENE_E { EXEC_E }
    def     { EXEC_U }
}

//CONCLUSION : Shorter, simpler, lightweight syntax
//                               => better vision of the execution workflow
"


t3 A perfect candidate : Enumerates

p As we said a few chapters before, the most important thing we have to remember from a data item for using it remains in its name. \
This is sometimes so important that, in some cases, we don't even require to know the value stored inside a data item for using it. \
The only thing we want is to have a certain number of names for differenciating them between each other, but their value doesn't really matter.

p This situation happens very frequently, basically, every time a selection have to be done. \
Let's see this in our previous example :
" z Figure 92
SCENE_A = `00 //the values given here does not really matter.
SCENE_B = `01 //we only need several items to differentiate.
SCENE_C = `02
SCENE_D = `03
SCENE_E = `04

byt scene = SCENE_C //select the scene we are currently in
                    //(never using raw values but only using names)

//rest of the code
...
"

p Using data items with arbitrary values as we did in the above example works perfectly. \
However, the Z also provides a ZCE specificly made for that kind of situation to simplify the thing : \benumerates\b. \
This is also a very common element that you can find in lots of other programming languages. \
Enumerates (or sometimes called "enums") are nothing more than regular data items except that their declaration and call are a bit shortened and optimized. \
Here is an example of how we declare and use enumerates in Z (same example as before) :
" z Figure 93
enm SCENE {
    A, B, C, D, E
}

//get selection
s = SCENE.C
swi s {
    SCENE.A { EXEC_A }
    SCENE.B { EXEC_B }
    SCENE.C { EXEC_C }
    SCENE.D { EXEC_D }
    SCENE.E { EXEC_E }
    def     { EXEC_U }
}
"

p As you can see, we use the DCL_ENM ZCI to declare an enumerate and their values are accessed using an \ufield access symbol\u (\m.\m). \
It is important to know that behind an enumerate, data items will be generated to fulfill the same role as in the previous versions of that example. \
Thus, each key of an enumerate is accessible as for regular data items. \
\b\uWarning :\u\b data items generated from enumerates are \bCONSTANT\b so you can only use them for \breading\b.

p Values associated to the keys of an enumerate are unsigned integers starting from 0. \
They are stored into data items of a certain type given in the compiler option ENUMERATES_KEYS_TYPE. \
If this option is set to \mauto\m, the compiler will automatically choose which type to use to save memory. \
For example, if there are less than 256 values, the keys can be stored on 1 byte of data so using \mubyt\m keys is enough.

p Enumerates can also be declared inside modules and so belong to it. \
This is quite the same thing as we do with regular data items basically. \
Here is a tiny example of it :
" z Figure 94
mod MyModule {
    enm SCENE { A, B, C, D, E }
}

//acces to values
a = ^MyModule.SCENE.A
b = ^MyModule.SCENE.B
...
"




t2 Loops

p Loops constitutes the second half of statements in general computing. \
Unlike conditions, they can appear in more various forms that are not always that obvious to understand \
depending on the programming language you are using. \
In Z, they are not that hard to manage but you will have some tiny words to remember to correctly deal with them.
b

t3 Basics

p When running a Z code, execution always go from the first ZCIs to the end. \
There is no other way to go back to a previous line except by using \uloops\u. \
Those loops can be defined by using 2 statements : "While" and "For".

p A WHILE statement is pretty similar to the very first IF statement we saw just before. \
It consists in using the keyword \mwhi\m followed by a condition and an execution block defined between braces includers :
" z Figure 95
whi CONDITION { DO_SOMETHING }
"

p The criterias for the condition and the execution are the same as for IF statements. \
However, there is no extension such as "els/elf" in while loops. \
The behavior of a while loop is quite simple : Depending on the condition, the execution block contained inside the braces includer is executed. \
(Until now, this seems to be the same thing as IF statements) \
The main difference is that execution will not only be done once but as many times as required until the condition becomes false.

p The more appropriate way of understanding a while loop is to consider that at the end of the executed block, execution goes back to the initial condition. \
Then, if the condition is still true, the execution block is processed one more time and so on. \
As soon as the condition becomes false, execution pursues after the statement to execute the rest of the code.

l Let's see a practical example :
" z Figure 96
count = `00

//repeat while count is lesser than 8
whi count < `08 {
    count = count + 1
}

//first time:  condition is true       => execute "count = count+1"
//second time: condition is still true => same thing
//...
//eighth time:  condition is now false (count is now equal to `08) => stop executing the loop
//                                                                    (pursuing as usual)
"

p There is nothing more to say about while statements for the moment. \
That was the easiest part of Z loops but not the most used ones. \
We will now take a look at FOR loops which are the most used loops in computing in general.


t3 Fixed iterativity

p For loops are quite similar to while loops. \
In one phrase, for loops are while loops including an iteration variable that is implicitely increased after each block execution. \
Originally, they are meant to iterate a fixed number of times in order to achieve an exact number of execution. \
However, through time and as more complex data structures came on, they have been extended into much more powerful statements.

p To create a for loop, we use the keyword \mfor\m followed by a name that will be our iteration variable and then what we will call "the header". \
This variable (which is a regular data item, I recall) is then, only accessible from inside the loop and in the loop header. \
In Z a for loop can be written using 4 different headers that we will see just now.
" z Figure 97
for v HEADER { //"v" is the name that will be taken for the iteration variable
    ...        //"HEADER" will depend on the way you want to use your FOR loop
    ...        //"v" is accessible inside loop content as well as inside the FOR header.
}
"

p The first header concerns a \urange\u and consists in 2 value ZCEs separated by a \urange symbol\u (\m:\m). \
The first value will determine the type as well as the initial value of the iteration variable. \
The second value will be cashted into the same type as the first one and corresponds to the last value our iteration variable will take. \
Also, those values can only be be \bprimitives\b.

p To do so, you may have guessed that, depending on whether the second value is greater or lesser that the first one, our iteration variable will increase or decrease. \
The iteration step is, by default, set to 1 (-1 if decreasing). \
However, an optional third value ZCE can also be added in the header (separated by another range symbol) to give a custom iteration step.
" z Figure 98
//second value greater than the first one => increasing sens
for a 5:9 {
    //"a" will take values 5,6,7,8,9
    //by the way, 5 is of type "int" so "a" is of type "int"
}

//second value lesser than the first one => decreasing sens
for b 4:-2 {
    //"b" will take values 4,3,2,1,-1,-2
}

//custom step
for c 4:18:2 {
    //"c" will take values 4,6,8,10,12,14,16,17,18
}



//using mixed types
for d in 4 : 18.3d : 2.4f {
    //"d" is still of type "int" (4 is an "int" value)
    // => 18.3d will be cashted into "int" (0x40324CCCCCCCCCCD on 64b => 0xCCCCCCCD on 32b => -858_993_459)
    // => 2.4f will be cashted into "int" (0x4019999a => 1_075_419_546)
    //Conclusion : DON'T USE MIXED TYPES
}



//using double floting point values only
for e in 4.0d : 18d : 0.1d {
    //"e" is a "dbl" (4.0 is a "dbl")
    //"e" will take values 4.0d, 4.1d, 4.2d, ... 18.0d
}
"
p \b\cff0000Please pay attention to type cashting !\c\b \
You can see in that example that some uncashted values are then implicitely transformed, leading to unexpected results... \
To avoid struggling with that I \b\cff0000highly recommend\c\b to use only values of the \u\cff0000same type\c\u in FOR headers.

p Actually, such loops are not mandatory in a Z code to work. \
Every FOR loop can be written into a WHILE equivalent. \
Those statements are more to see like shortcuts that real features to the code. \
Let's take a quick look at a while equivalent :
" z Figure 99
for a 4:16:2 {
    ....
}

//is (practically) equivalent to
a = 4
whi a <= 16 {
    ...
    a += 12
}
"
p By decomposing our FOR statement we see that actually it operates a "lesser or equal" comparison so that if the iteration variable goes too far, \
even after the second range value, the loop will stop. \
We can see this second value like "we must \bat least\b reach it".

p The second FOR loop header is pretty simple considering the first one we just saw : the "on" header. \
Using the keyword \mon\m followed by a value ZCE (still primitive), the compiler will consider the range as starting from 0 until the value given minus one. \
This is pretty useful when dealing with indexes because, you will see when coding that we always start counting by 0 in every data structure you will create. \
Another way to look at this FOR header is to consider that it will iterate a fixed number of times, the number you just mentionned :
" z Figure 100
ptr a = ``0011223344 //considering a certain amount of data (5 bytes here)

//practical use of "on" header for indexing
for p on 5 {
    //"p" will take values 0,1,2,3 and 4

    //we can write inside "a" at index "p"
    a[p] = `f8
}

//practical use of "on" header for just doing X times something
for p on 10 {
    doSomething() //as a result, will be called 10 times
}
"
p One thing that really differs from the \urange\u header is that you cannot set a custom step or trying to invert it using an ending value lesser than 0. \
\mon\m headers (as well as every other FOR header that we will see from now) are always \bincreasing\b with a step of \b1\b. \
By the way, this step is adapted to the type given for the iteration variable (\m`01\m for \mbyt\m, \m1s\m for \mshr\m...). \
This is mostly important for floating point values (\mflt\m => \m1f\m, \mdbl\m => \m1d\m).

p The third header of FOR loops is also very similar : the \min\m header. \
It is nothing more than an \mon\m header with a tiny difference on the value given as maximum : \
the compiler will not take it as is but rather look for a particular field named \mlength\m in that value. \
That means the value you will give must be a structure type that contains at least one field with that name. \
This kind of loop is really useful for iterating over collections of undeterminable length :
" z Figure 101
//declare a structure type "myStc"
typ myStc { byt length, ptr data = null }



//declare a structure item
s = myStc { 5, ``4433221100 }



//iterate IN s
for p in s { //equivalent to "for p on s.length {"
    s.data[p] = `0e
}
"

p For that last header, we will not only need a structure value with a \mlength\m field but also a specific operator to be declared first. \
The name of that last header is the OVER but we use the shorten keyword \movr\m to write it down. \
Infact, as we said this FOR loop will call a specific operator implicitely at each iteration. \
This one is the IIN operator (indexing index : "A[B]") made for the given structure and the type of an indexing variable (which will be the same as the \mlength\m field).

p You probably guessed what this FOR loop is aimed to do. \
The iteration variable of this one is not a primitive value as given before but a complete new element taken from the call of that indexing function. \
The principle is quite similar to the previous \min\m header because we also have a primitive data item increasing the same way (hidden, unaccessible by the way). \
However, the iteration variable is the result of an operation with the given structure and that increasing value.
" z Figure 102
//define some stuff for type "myStc"
typ myStc { byt length, ptr data = null }

byt [](myStc m, byt index) { //indexing the structure (with an index of type "byt")
    ret m.data[index]        // => indexing its "data" field
}



//declare a structure item
s = myStc { 5, ``00224488ff }



//iterate OVER s
for p ovr s {
    //p will take values s[`00], s[`01], s[`02], s[`03], s[`04]
    //    indexes are given with type "byt" because "s.length" is of type "byt"

    //in that case : `00,  `22, `44, `88, `ff
}

//it is equivalent to :
for tmp_p in s {
    byt p = s[tmp_p] //except "tmp_p" is not accessible
                     //in OVER headers
    //do stuff...
}
"
p This is it ! We finally reached the end of FOR loops. \
You might find it complicated at first sight because lots of things are hidden under very \ushort\u and easilly \uswitchable\u words. \
Welcome to the world of syntax design ! \
It is not that simple to find short keywords or syntaxes not too heavy but also explicit enough to allow anyone to understand it. \
Sometimes, we need to make choices and those are not done in 5 minutes. \
It can take hours, days or even months in some cases before finding a correct way of writting it.

p Here you have only 3 short human words to remember and the range header is using the \urange symbol\u (which is only used for that kind of purpose in Z). \
I think anyone can manage to learn that without too much difficulties. \
The tip is to start by the beginning, \urange\u header first, then \mon\m, and so on.

p We are arriving to the end of the real ZPL syntaxes now. \
At this moment, you \uare\u able to create your first program with your own types, functions, methods, modules, statements and more. \
The next chapter will tell us about some specific shortcut syntaxes that you might not use in your first programs in my opinion. \
However, those shortcut notations are pretty useful for dealing with more complex data structures easilly.




b
